<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h1">Character Classes</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q1">Will Everquest II have a class-based system?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Yes, as in the original game, characters will have a class which defines
much of their basic
ability and role in the world.  Unlike Everquest however, characters in
Everquest II will
progress through a series of classes as they advance.  This "class tree"
system will allow
characters to evolve in a different manner, and make decisions about which
end class they
wish to play as they progress through the game.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q2">How will the "class tree" work?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Each character will enter the world as a "generic class" adventurer.  Upon
reaching level
six, each character will be able to select one of five archetype classes:
Fighter, Mage,
Priest, Rogue, or Tradesman.  A choice will be required again at level 15,
and once again
at level 30.<br />
<br />
Some sites have reported that each character will be given the choice of
three additional
classes at levels 15 and 30, however this will require some overlap in the
class choices,
or for the class tree to be expanded to a total of (1+5+15+45) 66 classes.
So, either some
classes will allow fewer than three choices when branching out of them, or
certain career
paths will be able to reconnect to each other at a later level.  (The former
seems more
likely.)<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q3">When will the entire "class tree" be known?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The finalized version of the class tree will of course be available at
release.  Depending
on the nature of the Beta testing, it is possible that information about the
tree will be
available prior to that.  (NDA agreements are often lifted during late Beta
stages.)<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q111">Will there be limitations on which classes a character may
follow based upon their race?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Sadly (at least for those of us who prefer an unrestricted structure) yes,
characters will only be able to pursue certain classes based upon their
race.  Though class restrictions have not been finalized, and may not be
identical to those in Everquest, the original game can serve as some kind of
an example of likely class and race combinations.  Those of you who have
been (like me) dreaming of playing a Dwarf Enchanter, Halfling Bard, or
Troll Rogue should probably think of a different character concept.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q4">How many classes are there?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There are said to be a total of 48 classes at this time.  That means one
starting class,
five base classes, and some combination of classes to be selected at levels
15 and 30.
Those "end classes" selected at level 30 will most likely represent the
types of classes
Everquest players are familiar with.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q5">What specific classes can we expect to see?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The development team has been fairly quiet about which classes will exist,
however they
have stated that the following (most likely chosen at level 30) classes in
Everquest II are:
Assassin, Bard, Monk, Paladin, Pugilist, Ranger, and Shadow-Knight. In
addition, some
of the mid-level
classes are reported to be (obviously in the "fighter" tree): Warriors,
Crusaders and Brawlers.<br />
<br />
At this time, classes which are chosen at 30th level are known as
"sub-classes" in design-team-speak.  It is not entirely clear what
nomenclature will be applied to the various levels of the class tree.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q6">How will the Tradesman archetype and sub-classes work?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
First off, they will experience a different kind of game than the other four
archetypical
branches of the tree.  While you might delve into a dungeon with a sword,
bow, or spells to
defend yourself, a needle and thread are unlikely to impress the denizens of
darker Norrath.
Thus the skilled crafters within the Tradesman branch will be found in more
civilized areas,
plying their skill for the benefit of those more adventuresome characters
around.  Creating
and repairing items will be the primary skills of these non-combatants, and
they can expect
to be well-compensated for their efforts since tradeskills in Everquest II
were designed as
a core aspect of the game economy.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q7">Are there any specific details known about classes?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Very little is known about any of the classes at this point.  It has been
stated that the
players of Bards will no longer need exceptional dexterity and stamina in
order to twist
songs.  Instead this will be integrated into the mechanics of the character
class itself.<br />
<br />
Also within the Rogue tree will be an Assassin class.  They will focus on
the application of damaging strikes (backstabs) as well as poisons.  I can
only shudder at the raw damage potential of such a specialized class.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="#q104">Will characters be able to fill traditional group roles
regardless of their class?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Regardless of what specialization a character may choose, the general role
of their "archetype" should be fulfilled when they join a group.  After
experiencing a lot of grief over one class being chosen over another in
Everquest, players in Everquest II should be able to form groups much more
easily as a result.  As an example, all "priest" type classes will be able
to resurrect, and of course heal.<br />
<br />
The comparison drawn between melee "fighter" classes and magic-wielding
"mage" classes in a Q&A with Bill Trost reinforced the fact that the design
team has taken care to keep a balance between the roles of the various
archetypes.  Each of the four "adventurer" archetypes will have a definite
role within a group.  The majority of the time all four of the roles will be
needed in order to form an optimal group.  Thus while a group of four
"fighters" and two "priests" may not be unsuccessful, a group with two
"fighters", two "priests", a "rogue" and a "mage" might enjoy a greater
degree of success.  (Of course, in certain situations, having a particular
archetype present in a group may be almost mandatory.)<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h2">Character Information</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q8">What choices will be available when creating a
character?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
New characters will be created with the selection of race and gender, as
well as numerous
physical appearance options.  This detailed method of modifying a
character's appearance
will allow for a much greater variance between individuals when compared
with the original
Everquest method of simply choosing a face.  It is not known at this time if
any additional
choices will be offered during the generation process, but it is definite
that a new
character will no longer need to choose a class.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q9">Will characters be able to change their appearance
after creation?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
To some extent they will.  There have been reports that certain changes will
be possible, for
example to hair color or style.  What is definite is that clothing will have
an effect on a
character's appearance.  Since clothing will be "geometry" it is able to be
layered in order
to create different looks than in the original title.  Jewelery will not be
visible on a
character, and cloaks/hoods were pulled from the release of the game though
may appear in a
future expansion.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q10">What playable races will be available?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At present, 16 races are scheduled for release.  These races are: Barbarian,
Dark Elf,
Dwarf, Erudite, Froglock, Gnome, Half Elf, Halfling, High Elf, Human, Iksar,
Kerran, Ogre,
Ratonga, Troll, and Wood Elf.  Additional races may be released in the
future, though
nothing is known about possible expansions at this time.<br />
<br />
Two of the races have not been available as playable races in Everquest
prior to Everquest
II.  The Kerrans are replacements for the Vah Shir, whose moon is no longer
habitable.  The
Ratonga are a small (evil) rat-like race who are likely to be adept
rogues.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q11">How many levels will characters be able to attain in
Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At release, characters should be able to play as a member of any class all
the way to level
100 with ample content provided for that purpose.  Future expansions are
expected to increase
the level cap above 100, and reports indicate that the game mechanics freely
scale to far
above that initial cap, thus providing more expandability for years to come.
  Levels should
be easier to attain in Everquest II than they were in the original.  Rising
to level 100 is
supposed to be similar to rising to level 50 in Everquest.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q12">Will characters be able to quest to advance in
levels?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The new quest system provides many more options for those players who wish
to advance their
characters by following quests.  Much larger experience awards may be
anticipated, as well as
quests which may only be completed once by any given character.  As well,
the structure of
quests should be much more rigid, to insure that the proper steps are
followed when following
a quest from start to finish.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q13">Is it possible to transfer characters from Everquest
into Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There is no possibility of transferring characters.  The world of Everquest
II is 500 years
in the future, and exists in a kind of "parallel universe", thus characters
who exist in the
world of the original Everquest will not be able to adventure in the new
game.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q14">Are there any benefits available to people who choose
to play both Everquest and Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
It is possible to pay one subscription fee ($21.99) for all Sony Online
Entertainment games.
Thus one benefit is a lower price for each individual game.  In addition,
there have been
"cross-game" benefits discussed.  It is possible that specific zones and
quests will be
available within Everquest II (and possibly Everquest) exclusively to
subscribers of both
games.   There may be items which are exclusive to these dual-subscribers as
well.  It is
also possible that some method of communication or interaction with
individuals playing
within Everquest will be allowed.  The details of these benefits are vague,
though it is
apparent that the development team intends to provide play incentives to
current Everquest
players in order to draw them into the world of Everquest II.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h3">Combat</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q15">How will combat in Everquest II work?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The design team has attempted to make it an interactive experience when
characters engage in
combat.  Unlike the original, there will be more to combat than "hitting
auto-attack" and
pounding on a special move every so often.  It is expected that characters
will need to
choose locations on an opponent to focus attacks on, and that the choices
made in this
respect will influence their efficiency in the fight.  Attacking a location
which is more
heavily armored is likely to result in less deadly results than if a weaker
point is chosen
as the target.  There may in fact be more choices or actions which will need
to be performed
in a battle, though details remain sketchy at this time.<br />
<br />
This complexity will extend to combat spellcasting at least in part.  Though
specifics are vague, the development team has indicated that the role of a
spellcaster in combat will be more complicated.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q16">Will there be any special advantages to grouping up
to defeat foes in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There has been a bit of talk regarding the group-centric focus of Everquest
II.  SOE has
released the Everquest Online Adventures product for the Playstation 2
platform relatively
recently, and there is some speculation as to the possibilities of a
grouping system which
reflects the mechanics of that title being reflected in EQII.  If this is
the case, grouping
to kill creatures will be very profitable indeed in terms of the effort
required as compared
to the experience delivered.<br />
<br />
In addition, a major advantage to groups will be that much of the Everquest
II content is
expected to be "encounter" oriented.  Thus instead of fighting single
creatures, entire
groups of opponents will face adventurers on a regular basis.  Taking this
into account, it
seems that the largest advantage of grouping will be found in the ability to
withstand more
dangerous encounters.  In this respect it is similar to Everquest, however
with the added
incentive of a larger percentage of encounters being available which focus
on
multiple-opponent combat.<br />
<br />
Finally, there will exist an option to create a "super group" or "group of
groups" in order to
tackle raid-type encounters.  This should provide a much better mechanic for
handling large
assaults on major enemies than in the previous title.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q17">How will the skills and abilities of a character play
a part in battle?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Every class of character should have a general level of ability to lend to a
given combat
situation.  This is expected to be dependant on their "archetype" as well as
their sub-class.
In addition, players will have a certain amount of discretionary resources
allocated as their
alter-egos advance. These "points" are intended to allow a degree of
customization to be
applied to each character.  Thus two level 50 Bards may have a (somewhat)
different set of
abilities depending on how these resources were allocated as they
developed.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q18">Will there be a large number of diverse creatures to
combat?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The development teams has taken great pains to assure that not only are
there many kinds of
opponents available, but also that they blend in with the game environment
and behave
appropriately as well.  Thus a creature which is a snake should act like a
snake, rather than
behaving as if it were a goblin.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q19">Is the Artificial Intelligence featured in combat
going to be challenging?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Depending on the difficulty of the creature, it may simply stick to one
method of trying to
defeat characters fighting it.  However it may also cycle through a number
of different
methods in order to find one which works most effectively against those
fighting it.  As
well, whole groups of creatures are expected to work more as a team than
they did in the
original Everquest, creating a much more exciting (and dangerous) combat
experience.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q58">Will ranged combat be viable in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
While ranged combat is not expected to replace melee combat (unlike certain
other games), it is being looked at as a viable method of engaging
opponents.  There are a number of final classes which are expected to be
proficient in ranged combat.  Obviously this method of combat will be most
effective when characters engage the opponent and attract attention while
the missile specialist stands back.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h4">Communication</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q20">How will communication with other players take place
in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The majority of communication will be done via the User Interface (UI) chat
window.  Players
will be able to communicate on a number of chat channels, similar to the
original Everquest.
Most likely, private conversations will be available via this interface, as
well as
conversations with those nearby, your group, an entire zone, or whole a
guild.  There is also
a "chat bubble" effect which will cause words to appear over the heads of
those who are
speaking.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q21">How will characters interact with NPCs?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There is a new dialogue tree which will allow characters to choose from
available responses
when speaking to an npc.  So no more hunting for the right words as was
necessary in the
original we hope!<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q22">Can characters make gestures and emotes?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Yes, there are a wide variety of emotes and associated gestures available
within Everquest II.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q23">Will voice communication be available?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The development team has reported that they plan to provide voice support in
the game,
though it is not clear if this will be active upon release.  If it is
implemented, the
system will likely function within groups, as trying to voice chat with 100+
people in a
zone is not very efficient.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h5">Death and Binding</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q24">How will binding work in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Given the difficulties faced by "non-casters" in the original Everquest, the
design team
made the decision to incorporate numerous bind points which everyone may use
into Everquest
II.  The goal of seeding the zones with these locations is to insure that
you should
generally be no further than five minutes from your bind point.  It is
implied that each
character will have the ability to change their own bind point, though this
is not definite,
and the requirements (spell, coin, or something else entirely) to do so have
not been
disclosed.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q25">What will death be like?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The character will remain in their corpse after death and be faced with a
choice.  This
choice deals with how they wish to return the character to life.   The first
(encouraged and
expected to be most common) method will be to wait for resurrection.  The
second will be to
accept the sacrifice of a random item in inventory inn order to be returned
to one's bind
point with the rest of the character's gear intact.  This system is designed
to allow
characters to rely on each other's skills, while allowing for situations
which may require a
speedy resolution, or in which there is no hope of resurrection.  Once alive
again, the
character will suffer from an "experience debt" which will need to be
overcome before
(positive) experience may be earned again.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q26">How will the "experience debt" work?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
When a character dies, they will be assessed a penalty for a certain amount
of experience.
This amount may be "worked off" just by gaining experience as usual.  It
will also be
possible to allow the debt to fade over time.  It is not clear if this will
require a
character to be logged out, the entire account to be logged out, or simply
for time to
pass.  Thus there truly is no experience "loss", rather there is a temporary
setback.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h6">Economy</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q27">Will items wear out over time?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Items such as weapons and armor will slowly wear out through use.  At this
time it has been
stated that items appropriate to a given character will probably last the
character until
they outgrow them given periodic repairs.  Items will not lose efficiency
due to this
attrition, though they will eventually break if not cared for or used for
too long.  The
repairs required by this system are expected to be carried out by characters
adept in
tradeskills.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q28">Will it be possible to "twink" characters?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Two main factors act to minimize the impact of twinking in Everquest II.
First is the fact
that items wear out over time.  Thus taking "old" equipment and giving it to
other
characters will be less effective as it was in the original Everquest.  An
item which has
been used "as expected" by one character during their advancement may not
last long enough to
be used during the same period for a second character.  (Or may last through
an appropriate
period for more than two, depending on wear and tear or luck.)<br/>
<br />
Secondly, items will all be given a statistic which interacts with a
character's "knowledge"
trait.  If an item is too "difficult" for a character to use, there will be
a higher rate of
item wear as well as a decrease in the efficiency of the object.  Thus
taking a sword which
is appropriate to a level 50 character and giving it to a level 10 character
will not be
exceptionally effective, and will rapidly result in a broken sword.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q29">How will characters who are crafters affect the
economy?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Tradeskills in Everquest II have received a massive boost in complexity and
viability.
Player character tradesmen will be able to repair items which have become
damaged as well as
create items which are much more competitive with dropped items in power
level.  These
factors should cause those characters following the path of a tradesman to
hold a valuable
place in the Everquest II society.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q105">What is the currency system like in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As in Everquest, 1 platinum coin is equal to 10 gold coins or 100 silver
coins or 1000 copper coins.  Or 1PP=10GP=100SP=1000CP . . . start hoarding
those coppers!<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h7">Game Development and Engine Information</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q30">Is the development team the same for Everquest II as
it was for the original Everquest?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
No, some of the designers and workers on the original game have moved on to
work on different
projects both inside SOE as well as for other companies.  Some of the
original Everquest
development team are working on Everquest II, though the sheer scale of
creating the game has
required a larger number of team members.  Thus the development team is a
mix of the old and
the new.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q31">What 3D engine does Everquest II utilize?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The engine was developed for the Everquest II game itself.  It is was
designed with the
purpose of being an advanced, dedicated 3D MMORPG engine.  As such it has
many features which
enhance the role it was designed to play.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q32">Does the game engine require DirectX 9
hardware?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Only a DirectX 9 software compatable card is necessary.  A card which
supports the DirectX 9
hardware specifications will be capable of displaying those features of the
Everquest II
engine which require such technology.  In some cases these are
optimizations, thus a DirectX
9 card is (in general) more desirable than a DX8 cards.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q33">How are large numbers of characters and creatures
handled by the game engine?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The engine will be adjustable manually to allow a faster framerate (by
lowering detail)
during large raid situations or in crowded zones.  In addition, the
rendering process scales
by distance, so that when creatures or characters are far away, they are
drawn with less
detailed models using faster rendering techniques.  These aspects combined
should make even
crowded raids bearable, provided the appropriate steps are taken by the
player to configure
their system for such.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q34">What technologies and software programs are being
used in the development of the game?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
All the humanoid movements are being generated with the assistance of motion
capture
technology.  As far as software is concerned, the team is using Maya for
modeling, DPVS for
occlusion culling, Miles for sound and Granny for character animation
export.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h8">General Information</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q35">What is Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Everquest II (EQ2) is a new Massively Multiplayer Online Role Playing Game
(MMORPG) being
developed by Sony Online Entertainment (SOE) for modern computers running
the Microsoft
Windows operating system.  In the game players take on the roles of the
inhabitants of a
fantastic world called Norrath.  Developing their skills and advancing in
power, characters
may come to explore the most dangerous reaches of the lands, and to discover
secrets lost in
time.<br />
<br />
Following up the massively popular Everquest game which boasts over 500,000
subscribers
worldwide, Everquest II seems destined to make an enormous impact on the
world of MMORPGs.
With a tradition of success, SOE has developed Everquest II in an effort to
make an even
better game.  Using the "hands on experience" of running Everquest and
combining it with the
desire to "learn from past mistakes", the development team has worked hard
to create a game
which will (hopefully) appeal to both current and past Everquest
subscribers, as well as
players new to Norrath.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q36">Is Everquest II a sequel, or is it an expansion to
Everquest?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Actually Everquest II is neither a true sequel, and it is definitely not an
expansion.  The
game takes place in kind of an "alternate future" of Everquest, a kind of
"what could be"
look at 500 years into Norrathian future.  Thus it exists as a sort of
"parallel universe"
and doesn't really follow the events and history of Everquest.  As the
original Everquest is
not required to play Everquest II, the game is not an expansion in any
way.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q37">What is the setting of Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The game takes place in a "parallel universe", a kind of alternate future of
the current
Everquest title.  Set 500 years in the future (in a time known as the Age of
Destiny), the
world of Norrath has been struck by a number of catastrophies which have
left the face of the
planet (and various objects associated with it) forever changed.  The game
will be familiar
to players of the original Everquest, however it will also contain many
aspects which are
quite unfamiliar, thus providing plenty to explore and experience for
veterans.  The cities
of Freeport (now overrun by evil) and Qeynos (one of the last bastions of
good) provide new
adventurers with a base of operations as they explore the strange and the
strangely familiar.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q38">What is an MMORPG?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
A Massively Multiplayer Online Role Playing Game is a computer program which
allows a huge
number of people to come together in one virtual location and assume the
roles of various
different types of characters.  Typically MMORPGs allow hundreds, thousands,
or even tens of
thousands of people to adventure within the same "server" simultaneously.
This type of
graphical game has been gaining popularity in recent years, though text-only
versions of
such games have been available for well over a decade.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q39">What is the goal of an MMORPG, and can you win
one?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There is no set goal to an MMORPG save those determined by the individual
player.  Some
players wish to gain wealth, some wish to advance in power, and others
simply to socialize
and play their character's role to the fullest.  Consequently there is no
real victory to
such a game.  Unlike a typical computer game there is no ending where the
"final boss" is
defeated.  Playing an MMORPG can take an almost limitless amount of time as
a result.  Many
Everquest players have logged on regularly since the release of that game
and have acrued
play time in the hundreds of days.  When compared to the average time to
defeat a typical
video game (20-80 play hours), the replayability and longevity of an MMORPG
becomes obvious.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q40">Can I enjoy Everquest II if I don't have as much time
to play as others?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The design of the game focuses on meeting the needs of as many types of
players as possible.
While there will always be people with many hours each day to play the game,
there are also
many others who do not have that time to invest.  Everquest II has been
designed with this in
mind, and should provide those playing for relatively short periods of time
with ample
enjoyment, while still allowing those who choose to play long sessions as
much pleasure as
the original.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q41">Do you have to pay a monthly fee in order to
play?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Yes, in addition to the purchase price of the software, you will need to pay
a monthly access
fee.  At this time the fee has not been set.  There is an option to pay a
combined fee for
all SOE games.  That charge will be $21.99, so for players of the original
Everquest it may
be worth it to pay the one rate instead of individual subscriptions for each
game.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q42">Once released, will there be regular updates to
Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As with the original Everquest, there will be updated content, mechanics
fixes, and bug
fixes.  There are also plans to create expansions for the game, though the
first of these
will likely not follow until a considerable time after release.  If
Everquest II is as
successful as the original, expansions should follow at regular intervals
for years to come.
Each expansion will need to be purchased.  Free updates will generally be
for smaller things,
single zones, new quests, and new spells and items as needed.  A dedicated
team will handle
the "EQ2Live" issues.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q107">Will it be possible to play more than one character?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
An Everquest II account will allow multiple characters to be player.  The
dev team has released the information that the number of characters allowed
per account on each server will be more than one.  The exact number is not
known at this time.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h9">Guild Information</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q43">Will there be player-run guilds in Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As in the original Everquest, there will be the opportunity for players to
create their own
guilds.  The process through which guilds are created has not been announced
yet.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q44">How will guilds be structured?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There has been no solid information and precious few hints dropped by the
development team in
regards to guilds.  It has been made to sound as if there will be more
features to guilds in
Everquest II than there were in the original, however this is too vague to
say definitively.<br/>
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q102">Will good and evil characters be able to join the same
guild?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
It is apparent that the question of differing types of characters joining
one guild will be up to the Guild Leader.  It is not known if some method of
setting various options will be allowed.  It is possible that some kind of a
template could be set up to add hardcoded "rules" to the guild in order to
avoid "undesirable" characters.  This is all merely supposition at this
point.  The simple answer is: yes.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q103">Is there any danger to joining a guild (or other
organization) which includes individuals of an opposing alignment?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
In part, yes.  Within Everquest II the design team made a concious decision
to cause faction to be a more important and omnipresent thing.  As a result,
common activities which Everquest guilds engaged in may become more
difficult to successfully pull off should an Everquest II guild attempt to
do so.  For example, raiding the home plane of one's own God is more likely
to be a bad plan in Everquest II.  As a result, guilds may find that
sticking more closely to alignment lines for their member pool is much
easier.  It is not known at this time how difficult changing one's basic
factions enough to become a member of the "other side" will be.  (It will
probably involve more than a few hundred bottles of milk though.)<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q101">Will there be other types of social structures
available?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Yes, there have been hints dropped about organizations beyond guilds.  What
precisely these organizations may be is debatable.  At one point something
was mentioned about some kind of adventuring fellowship, which may be a sort
of a mini-guild made up of people who group together often.  Only time will
tell what more may be in store.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h10">Housing Details</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q45">Will players be able to own houses in Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There will be two types of housing available to players.  The first type is
expect to be
relatively inexpensive and will use a "pocket zone" to create an unlimited
number of player
"houses" (which are really more like inn rooms, or apartments).  The second
will be plot
houses, which will be visible as individual entities and will be much more
expensive, and
limited in number.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q46">By owning a house, will a character have more storage
space?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
According to the development team it will be useful to own a house in order
to gain a larger
amount of space in which to store goods.  How much storage space will be
afforded by houses,
or if that space will depend on what type of housing a character owns is
unknown at this
time.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q47">Can guilds open up guildhall facilities?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There will be a limited number of buildings for guilds available.  It is not
known if the
"pocket zones" will be used as guild facilities, but seems unlikely.  It is
expected that
guild halls will be available to only those guilds which attain a large
amount of wealth and
have a sizable member body.  Prices on prime guild real-estate are expected
to be
astronomical.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q48">Will houses or guild structures require a monthly
"rent" to be paid on them?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At this time it is not known if any of the housing or guildhall options
available will
require a monthly fee to be paid in order to keep them active.  As a "money
sink" option,
this would seem a viable option, however it may be that the development team
expects the
economy to be balanced enough to not need anything more than a single
purchase price for
housing or guildhalls to be paid.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q112">Will any "unusual" types of houses be available?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
While the design team has discussed possible "houseboats" being allowed, it
is unlikely that such a feature will exist at release.  Perhaps during a
future expansion more "exotic" houses will become available.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q49">Are there going to be towns created by player
characters in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Player-built towns are expected to be released in a future expansion, or as
additional
content.  The development team felt that the design for the system was too
ambitious to be
included in the release of the title.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h11">Magic System</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q50">What will magic be like in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
In general, there will be a "familiar feel" to the Everquest II magic
system.  The new magic
system will require casters to manage their mana as usual, but will also use
"concentration"
as a resource.  In addition, it is possible that even more spells will
require items in order
to cast, as is the case with "pet spells" in Everquest.  As in Everquest, it
will be possible
to gain skill in casting spells, though it is not known if this will be
based upon the
individual spell, spell types, or perhaps even spell lines.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q51">Will casters need to buy their spells?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Advancing in level will provide casters with a basic knowledge of their
spells.  In addition
to this basic spellset, it is possible to gather additional spells through
other means.
Quests and research (crafting) are possible sources which have been
mentioned, however it
remains possible that certain spells will be available for purchase as well.
  It is
relatively certain that casters will be less bound by having to carry
scrolls with them as
they near a new circle.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q52">What kinds of "spell effects" can be expected in
Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The new engine allows for a "particle" system to be implemented.  This
system makes it
possible for persistent visible effects for certain spells, as well as
effects with more
"substance" than in the first Everquest.  In general, the spell effects will
be much more
sophisticated than those found in previous MMORPGs, though the engine does
not support
effects which deform the terrain.  Another new effect which promises to be
impressive is the
fact that characters will supposedly "morph" between forms when they use a
shape-changing
(such as wolf-form in Everquest) spell.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q53">How common will "resurrection" spells be?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
While in Everquest it required a relatively high level Cleric to perform
"resurrection" type
spells, the death system in Everquest II required some changes to this.
Multiple classes
will have access to a resurrection line of spells, and characters will
receive those spells
at an earlier point.  As such there should be less demand on a single class
to deal with
resurrections, which are likely to be in much higher demand starting at low
levels given the
new death system.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h12">Player Versus Player</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q54">Is Everquest II a PvP game?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The core design of the game revolves around PvE (Player Versus Environment)
combat.  The
design team has indicated that a form of PvP will exist on all servers which
is known as
Indirect Player Versus Player (IpvP), though this system will not allow
players to fight each
other directly.  In addition it is expected that both duels and arenas will
exist in some
form within the game.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q55">Will there be PvP servers for Everquest II?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
It is the intention of the team to create specific servers with rulesets
balanced towards
PvP.  What type of PvP this will entail is unknown at this time, though with
the obvious
divide between the forces of "good and evil", it seems logical that a team
PvP ruleset may be
developed.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q56">Are there plans to have a team working specifically
on Pvp?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
A separate team for PvP is not likely to be formed.  The Everquest II team
will handle
issues in the released game as needed, PvP problems included.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h13">Release Information</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q57">When will Everquest II be released?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The game will arrive when it is ready, and hopefully not sooner.  Anyone who
has experienced
the launch of an MMORPG before can attest to the fact that the more issues
the developers
address before the game goes live the better off everyone is.  When
Everquest was released,
the instability in the servers was so extreme that it took multiple weeks
before the game
was truly playable for prolonged stretches of time.  People who joined
during the first two
weeks were given two additional free weeks in order to make up for the
difficulties they
experienced.  Puts a slight crimp in plans to explore and enjoy a new game
when you can't
maintain a connection for longer than half an hour, and log back in to find
yourself dead,
doesn't it!  (Of course, this is vastly preferable to what happened when
Anarchy Online was
launched and it was almost impossible to move!)<br />
<br />
At present, Everquest II has a release date of Q4 2003, which is generally
being reported by
retailers as early to mid-November 2003.  The development team has continued
to report that
the schedule is being maintained, and that the game will be ready for
release "on-time" in Q4
2003.  One must always remember that Beta testing can uncover issues which
were not detected
earlier in development and testing, thus can cause delays in the release of
a product up
until the last minute.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q58">Will Everquest II be released in North America and
Europe (or Australia, etc.) simultaneously?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At this time the actual release schedule for different regions is unknown.
SOE will handle
distribution in North America, while UbiSoft will be handling European
distribution.  No
details have been forthcoming on how distribution to other regions will be
handled, nor has
it been confirmed that the game will see a simultaneous release.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h24">Religious Details</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q109">What will the religious "climate" be like in Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As the setting of Everquest II is 500 years into the alternate future of
Norrath, religion may very well prove changed by events.  One of the major
"historical factors" involved is a war which has been fought between the
gods.  As such, certain sects which existed in the original title may have
completely vanished, or at the very least transformed into something almost
unrecognizable.<br />
<br />
Religion remains in general a mystery at this point.  It is certain that
whatever else may be true, there will be a great deal of information to be
uncovered regarding the backstory of Everquest II once the game is
released.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q110">Will religion play an important role in Everquest II?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
While factions have played a part in the gameplay of the original Everquest,
the extent to which religion affected a character was minimal.  It took
around two years for the first "god restricted" items to appear, along with
various quests with religious backstories.  Unlike the first title,
Everquest II seems likely to focus strongly on the different factions within
Norrath, including the religious ones.  The choice of who to worship or what
to believe in should have more impact on the average character.  This is
something which should bring more "roleplaying" to the game in the long
run.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h14">Server Details</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q59">Are game servers going to be available by
region?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There are plans for servers based on both coasts of the United States as
well as some
located in Europe.  The number and specific locations of these servers have
not been
announced, though the development team has indicated that as release draws
closer there will
be more information on this subject forthcoming. <br />
</br>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q60">Will the servers be named like the original Everquest
servers?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At present, server names have not been disclosed.  The names of the servers
will probably
remain indeterminate until shortly before release.<br />
</br>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q61">Are there plans for servers with different
rulesets?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Player Versus Player (PvP) rulesets will most likely be used on a small
number of servers.
The majority of servers should be subject to the standard rules.  It is not
known what types
of PvP servers will be available, though the definate split between "good
and evil" within
Everquest II may make team PvP more viable than in the original game.
The development team has stated that they expect at least one PvP
server to be available at release.<br />
<br />
There have been reports of Roleplay (RP) servers being released as part of
the initial launch.  There are almost no details other than sketchy rumors
at this time.  What is proven is that other MMORPGS have had successful RP
servers, and that there are appreciable numbers of Everquest II hopefuls
with interest in such servers.  It is not known if anything other than a
tag will be used to differentiate such servers.  While there are compelling
reasons for PvP rulesets being different, the differences made on an RP
server would be primarily rooted in the attitudes of the players populating
them.<br />
</br>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h15">Skills</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q62">What types of skills will Everquest II characters
posess?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There is more information that is unclear than known at this point regarding
skills.  There
have been suggestions of the skills system made, but for the most part no
solid information
exixts.  Supposedly characters will have three major "attributes" which
affect their skills:
Arts, Knowledge, and Technique.  Arts determines how efficiently basic
functions such as
foraging and kicking are performed.  Knowledge affects what sort of
equipment may be used
effectively by a character.  Technique is a measure of how well weapons and
such may be
employed.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q63">Will tradeskills be available in Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Yes, but there's a bit of mixed news on that front.  Tradeskills will
finally be truly viable
and have been treated as a core element of the game as opposed to some kind
of "extra" which
takes a back seat to hacking and slashing.  However, due to the fact that
they are viable,
normal "adventurers" will not be able to master them as they were able to in
the original.
Instead, a new "archetype" known as Tradesman has been created and endowed
with the ability
to craft and repair items.  Thus these skills will be available only to
those who choose to
follow a non-combatant role within Norrath.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q106">How will tradeskills work?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Unlike the simple (yet sleep-inducing) inventory manipulation and "clicking
combine" of the Everquest system, tradeskills should prove to be much more
interesting in Everquest II.  This is a good thing considering players are
expected to dedicate themselves to playing tradesmen!  While not much is
known about the system itself, it is expected to take more skill and less
mindless clicking, as well as being fun.  We can only hope!<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q113">Will there be any support or secondary skills involved in
tradecraft?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The gathering of materials for tradeskills will be done through a set of
skills which all characters will have access to.  This should allow for some
low-level economy at the very least, as tradesmen pay adventurers and each
other to go harvest raw materials.  This harvesting (supplemented by
components which might be available from defeated creatures, or from NPC
merchants) is likely to be the main method of gaining materials for many
tradeskills.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q98">What are "discretionary points" and how do they affect
skills?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The system for these points comes into effect after the character's final
class is selected at level 30.  Each character may utilize these points in
order to increase their skill caps.  The precise number of such points is
not known, but it seems logical that these points would allow for a kind of
"specialization" for all characters, beyond what is provided in Everquest
presently.  While one Ranger might find themselves more adept with a bow,
another could be the more skilled tracker, and yet another could wield two
weapons with greater skill.  This system seems likely to make only a
moderate amount of difference overall, though will provide for slight
advantages when certain skills which have been increased come into play.<br
/>
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q99">How will skills be improved?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As in the original Everquest, the majority of skill development will be done
through use.  How closely this system will mimic that of the first game is
unclear.  In order to become a master of all their relevant skills,
characters may find that they need a fair amount of practice in using them.
If this is the case, certain old Everquest methods of skill improvement may
see popularity in Everquest II.  (Switching to junk weapons just to train,
casting low-power spells over and over, etc.)<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h16">Sound Details</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q64">What will the music in Everquest II be
like?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
For the new game, SOE has comissioned Laura Karpman (whose website may be
found at
<a href="http://www.laurakarpman.com/">www.laurakarpman.com</a>) to score
the soundtrack.  The
development team has been working closely with her in order to integrate the
music into the
flow of the game.  Combat tracks are expected to be more dynamic and
appropriate than in the
original game as well.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q65">Will players have the option to play their own music
files?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There will be optional playback of MP3 files incorporated into the engine.
This feature will
be similar to that which is offered in the current Everquest game.
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q66">Are instruments in the game capable of producing
sounds that other players can hear?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Other than some associated animations, playing an instrument will have no
effect on the
experience of other players.  Bards will just have to stick to their bad
poetry!<br />
<br>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q67">Will there be surround sound available?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
While sound effects will be played back using surround technology, music
will not be.  Thus
you may hear footsteps to the rear, but the haunting melodies associated
with Nektulos
Forest will be playing only in standard stereo.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h17">Storage</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q68">Will there be personal storage available for excess
items?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As in Everquest there will be banks available to store your excess goods in.
  However, these
banks will be localized, and instead of being able to access your storage
facility from any
bank across the world, you will need to visit the town in which your goods
are stored.  In
addition to the banking storage system, there will be an opportunity to
purchase personal
housing which will provide some (currently undisclosed) manner of additional
storage space.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q69">What are the possible issues players may experience
with this new storage system?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There has been information released to indicate that moving one's personal
items from one
storage facility to the next may involve the need to acquire wagons, carts,
or other such
transport.  There may be an opportunity to hire guards to protect the
"caravan" as it travels
from one location to the next, and it is possible that attacks on the
transports may occur
which will need to be dealt with.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q70">How much storage space will be available to a
character?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Inventory space in Everquest II is limited by volume as well as weight.  It
is unknown at
this time as to what constraints will be placed upon storage in banks or
personal housing.
It is possible that both these factors will be taken into account for
personal storage, which
could have impact on the ability of people to store large amounts.  This
will be most likely
to impact individuals in the tradeskill classes and those who "play
merchant" frequently.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h18">System Specifications</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q71">What are the system requirements for Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The minimum requirements for the game are:<br />
OS: Windows 98/2000/ME/XP<br />
Processor Speed: 1.0GHz or faster<br />
Memory: 512Mb or more<br />
Video: A DirectX 9, pixel/vertex shader compatible card with 64Mb or greater
texture memory<br />
Sound: DirectSound compatible card<br />
Internet: 56.6k or Broadband<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q72">Will a system meeting the minimum requirements really
play the game?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As with all modern games, the system requirements are meant to indicate the
minimum
specifications capable of running the game in a playable state.  With a
system that meets the
requirements, you should be able to play Everquest II, though the game will
need to be
configured to run with less detail and in a smaller resolution than systems
which have
greater specifications.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q73">Does the game engine really tax the system so much
that a "bleeding edge" system will slow down?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At E3 the systems showing the demo of Everquest II were seen to visibly slow
at times.  It is
a safe assumption to state that the systems running the game were
high-powered.  While the
game is likely not optimized for speed at this time, the in-game screenshots
which have been
provided by the development team clearly show a massive level of detail and
number of effects
are available.  In addition, environmental effects such as particles of dust
falling from
walls have been reported.  The combination of the effects, level of detail,
high-polygon
models, and high resolutions available mean that it is unlikely that any
current system will
be able to fully handle the Everquest II engine at top settings.<br />
<br />
The development team has indicated that over time, as new technologies
become available, the
game should scale to make use of the features of that hardware.  It is
important to bear in
mind that with Everquest the game engine was dated before release and was
never designed to
handle longterm play.  The engine for Everquest II is modern and was
designed with longevity
in mind.  For those interested in the game, a powerful system should prove
an excellent
investment.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q74">Will there be a version of the Everquest II client
for the Macintosh, or Linux platforms?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There are no current plans for an Everquest II client on Mac or *ix. The
release of a
Macintosh client for Everquest may be used to gather data as to the
profitability of such a
version for Everquest II.  A version for Linux is less likely, though one
can not say for
certain that it is impossible!<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h19">Testing</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q75">When will Beta testing for Everquest II
start?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Summer 2003 is the estimated Beta start date, however nothing further is
known.  The
developers have recently been using the term "soon" when asked when the Beta
will begin.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q76">Who will be admitted to the Beta?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Preference will be given to longtime Everquest Legends subscribers, then
longtime Everquest
subscribers.  The developers have stated previously that they hope to have
some inexperienced
players involved in the Beta as well as those who have been involved with
the Everquest game
for a considerable period of time.  The selection process will likely focus
on the needs of
the development team during each phase of the testing process.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q77">How many phases will be involved in the Beta
testing?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
At this time little is known about the actual stages involved in the
Everquest II Beta
process.  At least two Beta stages are likely however.  First will be a
limited Beta
involving specific individuals deemed neccessary to the testing of the game.
  The second is
an open Beta, which is likely to happen shortly before release.  In addition
to these
stages, it seems logical that there will be a gradual widening of the Beta
selection in
between.  It is typical for a game such as Everquest II to have three or
four stages in
addition to an open Beta.  Some of these stages may take place privately and
consist of
individuals approached by the development team and internal Quality
Assurance personnel.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q78">Will there be an Open Beta?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As mentioned above it is likely that there will be some kind of Open Beta.
Details of this
stage of testing are not known at this time.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q79">How can I apply to be a Beta tester?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The <a href="http://www.everquest2.com/">official Everquest 2 website</a>
will have more
details regarding Beta applications and the testing process "soon".  In
addition, community
sites such as Conclave of Guilds will be sure to spread the word when Beta
applications are
being accepted.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h20">Transportation</a><br/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q80">Will it be possible to ride a horse or other
mount?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There are plans for player-owned mounts within Everquest II.  It is
currently expected that
mounts will not be available upon release.  At a later point (most likely
when an expansion
is released) the development team plans to implement this form of
transport.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q81">Are ships going to be owned by players?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The development team has stated that they plan to allow ships to be owned by
players and used
for travel.  The plans also include encounters centered around ships,
opening up the
possibilities of sea monsters, pirates or other dangers.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q82">How will players move their items from one bank to
another?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Currently there are plans for the implementation of wagons or carts which
may be hired to
carry excess baggage between towns.  In addition, it may be possible to hire
NPC mercenaries
in order to protect such a caravan.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q83">How will teleportation work in Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Unlike the far-ranging spells featured in the original, teleportation spells
will be more
controlled in Everquest II.  Typically spells will take characters from in a
dungeon (danger)
to just outside (safety), or from one teleportation location to another.  In
this manner it
is expected that the teleportation abilities of certain classes will not
cause as much of a
disparity as in Everquest.  As well, there has been talk of every character
having a low
power teleportation spell, perhaps similar to gate.  This is, however, quite
unconfirmed.<br />
<br />
NPCs capable of teleporting characters were shown at E3, however the
development team has
indicated that this will not be commonplace within the game.  There will be
a limited number
of NPCs capable of teleporting characters, however they will be used for
very specific
purposes.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h21">User Interface (UI) Specifics</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q84">What will the UI look like?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The interface has been designed with a clean, space-saving look in mind.
The UI windows will
dock to the side of the screen, and many of the functions will be accessable
from a "toolbar"
which will auto-hide itself if desired.  All in all, it should provide an
attractive yet
functional and customizable interface which leaves most of the screen
available for viewing
the incredible world of Norrath.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q85">Will it be possible to manipulate the UI?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
As the UI is based upon XML, it should be readily modifiable.  The
development team
is not using the
same schema as in Everquest, however they do expect results of UI
modification by the players
to be very successful.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q86">Is there a compass available on-screen?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Yes, the development team decided that having people be able to find their
way around was a
positive thing, and thus have taken pity on all those who suffered the agony
of hitting their
"sense heading" hotkey so many thousands of time in the original Everquest.
It is expected
that the compass will be hidden when not needed in order to save screen
real-estate.  By some reports, completion of a newbie quest will be required
in order to unlock this compass.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q87">What is a "Weapon Configuration Menu"?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Something mentioned in an interview with E.J. Moreland (EQII Mechanics Lead)
which can only
be speculated about at this time.  It is very likely something within the UI
that allows a
player quick control over the weaponry or fighting tactics being used at any
given time. As
combat should require more in the way of tactics within Everquest II, this
item is probably
the UI expression of the player mechanics.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h22">Websites</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q88">What is the Official Everquest II website?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The official site may be found at <a
href="http://www.everquest2.com/">www.everquest2.com</a>.
This site is part of the SOE station.com site, and is updated with
information related to
Everquest II as it becomes available.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q89">What other websites exist for Everquest II
information?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Aside from the official website and Conclave of Guilds, there are many sites
which contain
information related to Everquest II.  The best resource for locating such
sites is typically
a search engine, such as <a
href="http://www.google.com/">www.google.com</a>.  The number of
sites dedicated to or providing information on Everquest II is growing every
day.  Information on other locations may also be found elsewhere on the CoG
web site.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q90">Which websites did the information in this FAQ come
from?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The <a href="http://www.everquest2.com/">offical site</a>, plus other fan
and news sites:
<a href="http://eq2.stratics.com/">Everquest 2 Stratics</a>,
<a href="http://www.eqii.com/">Everquest II: The Age of Destiny</a>,
<a href="http://www.homelan.com/">HomeLan</a>,
<a href="http://www.rpgvault.com">RPG Vault</a>, and <a href="http://www.eqsquare.com/">EQ Square</a>.
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_header">
<a name="h23">Zones</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q91">What zones will be available at release?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
There will be a wide variety of zones upon release. These will range from
familiar "old"
zones updated to account for the 500 years of history which have come to
pass, to brand new
"never before seen" zones designed exclusively for the release of Everquest
II.<br />
<br />
The continents of Velious and Kunark will not be available, though Odus,
Antonica and
Faydwer will.  Exploration of the world is intended to take place outwards
from the cities
of Qeynos and Freeport, where all new characters will enter the world.  The
forces of good
have rallied in Qeynos, while the hordes of evil have overrun Freeport.  The
difficulty of
zones is likely to be relevant to their distance from these two bastions of
"civilization"
at least initially.<br />
<br />
Those zones which are familiar from Everquest have been (in most cases)
enlarged and (in all
cases) redesigned to utilize the power of the new Everquest II game engine.
The development
team expects even the most visited areas of Everquest to spark new interest
in players when
they begin to explore the new game.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q92">What types of terrain can be expected in Everquest
II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Just as the original, Everquest II will feature a wide variety of
landscapes.  From forests
to mountains, deserts to plains, and islands to deep ruins, all manner of
obstacles await
characters venturing into the Norrath of 500 years later.  The game has been
developed from
the bottom up and each environment promises to be spectacular and new.  It
is worth
mentioning one specific terrain type, and that is water.  Since player-owned
ships are
expected to be available in Everquest II, water-covered zones will likely
play an even more
interesting role than in the original game.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q108">What will the weather be like in Everquest II zones?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
A wide variety of weather patterns should exist.  These will be based upon
the climate of the zone as well as the season.  While Freeport may get rain
at times, it will not see snow, even during winter.  As someone who enjoyed
snow a great deal in the original title, I'm looking forward to seeing the
new snow effect.  On a related note, the Norrathian winter season is known
as Decay and consists of four months: Firstchill, Deadening, Deepice, and
Grayeven.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q93">How large are the zones in Everquest II?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
The zones will vary in size depending on their purpose.  There will be tiny
"pocket zones"
such as those used for player housing, to very extensive (mostly) outdoor
areas.  The
development team has tried to create some very impressive locations to
explore so it seems
likely that we can expect a number of zones which are at least as large as
some of those
released in the expansions to the original Everquest, and quite likely
larger.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q94">What are "pocket zones" and "instancing"?</a><br
/>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Everquest II will support small "private" sub-areas which will be accessable
via various
means.  In some cases they will be triggered by a group entering the final
section of a
larger dungeon, while in others it will happen as a result of a quest.  The
idea behind the
"instancing" feature of EQII is to provide the possibility of completing
quests, raids, and
end-dungeon encounters without being disturbed by other individuals or
groups.  Groups
entering into an instanced area will need to do so together, as it will not
be possible to
"join" people who have moved into an instanced zone.  This technology should
allow for less
friction between players and more equitable sharing of the available
encounter areas.  The
extent to which "instancing" will be used in the game remains to be seen,
though the
development team expects it to have a signifigant impact on gameplay.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q95">Will you be able to "zone to safety" as in
Everquest?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
Despite initial gameplay plans and persistent rumors to the contrary, you
will indeed be able
to flee from combat by zoning.  The designers have made the decision to
leave this
"traditional" Everquest method of withdrawl intact in Everquest II.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q96">Will there still be time to get a snack while
zoning?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
One of the largest concerns of the zone system has been the amount of time
it took to do so
when compared to other MMORPGs.  As such, the development team has indicated
that they've
made great strides in the battle to combat zone loading times.  The delays
involved in zoning
within Everquest II should be much more tolerable.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_question">
<a name="q97">Will zones "evolve" over time?</a><br />
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="faq_answer">
<br />
It has not been officially stated that zones themselves will evolve and
change throughout the
game.  Reading between the lines of some interviews, it would seem that
given the "dynamic"
nature of some features in the game the development team does plan to make
changes over
time.  That may mean permanent changes such as have taken place in Everquest
(adding undead
outside Rivervale at night, etc),  temporary events (such as undead invading
in the hills of
Qeynos), or some different method entirely remains to be seen.<br />
<br />
<br />
</td>
</tr>
